{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network, directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, is a masterful exploration of ambition, betrayal, and the birth of an empire. released in 2010, the film is a fictionalized account of the founding of facebook and the legal battles that followed, based on ben mezrich’s book *the accidental billionaires*. it’s a gripping, fast-paced drama that blends sharp dialogue with a haunting score by trent reznor and atticus ross.\n\nthe story centers on mark zuckerberg (played with icy intensity by jesse eisenberg), a harvard computer prodigy whose creation of \"thefacebook\" revolutionizes social networking but also sets off a storm of litigation and personal fallout. the narrative unfolds through a series of flashbacks and courtroom scenes, revealing how zuckerberg’s genius and ruthlessness alienate those closest to him—his best friend eduardo saverin (andrew garfield), the winklevoss twins (armie hammer in a double role), and his former girlfriend erica albright (rooney mara).\n\njesse eisenberg’s performance is nothing short of electric. he captures zuckerberg’s social awkwardness, intellectual arrogance, and simmering resentment with unsettling precision. andrew garfield shines as eduardo, whose betrayal by zuckerberg forms the emotional core of the film. the supporting cast is equally stellar: armie hammer delivers a compelling dual performance as the privileged, entitled winklevoss twins, while brendan fraser and justin timberlake add depth in supporting roles. even minor characters like max mindy (rashida jones) and sean parker (timberlake) leave a lasting impression.\n\naaron sorkin’s script is a marvel of wit and precision, packed with rapid-fire exchanges that crackle with intelligence. lines like *\"you’re going to go through life thinking girls don’t like you because you’re a nerd. but it’ll be because you’re an asshole\"* drips with sorkin’s signature blend of humor and bite. the film’s pacing is relentless, mirroring the frenetic energy of silicon valley in the late 2000s.\n\nvisually, the social network is a feast for the eyes. david fincher’s direction is sleek and controlled, with a cold, blue-tinted aesthetic that underscores the film’s themes of isolation and ambition. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth is razor-sharp, while the editing by kirk baxter and angus wall keeps the story moving at a breakneck speed. trent reznor and atticus ross’s score—a mix of haunting electronic and ambient sounds—adds an almost mythic quality to the proceedings, elevating the film beyond a simple biopic.\n\none of the film’s greatest strengths is its refusal to glorify its protagonist. zuckerberg is not a hero; he’s a flawed, often unlikable figure whose genius is inseparable from his cruelty. the social network doesn’t just tell the story of facebook’s creation—it’s a meditation on the cost of success, the loneliness of power, and the way ideas can corrode human relationships.\n\nthe film’s legacy is undeniable. while it takes liberties with the facts (a point fincher has acknowledged), it captures something essential about the digital age: the way technology reshapes society, the ruthlessness of innovation, and the hollow victory of \"winning\" at the expense of everything else. it’s a film that feels as relevant today as it did in 2010, if not more so in an era dominated by social media and billionaire tech moguls.\n\nin short, the social network is a near-perfect film—smart, stylish, and scathingly funny, with a core of melancholy that lingers long after the credits roll. it’s a dissection of ambition, friendship, and the dark side of the american dream, all wrapped in a razor-sharp drama that never lets up. if you somehow haven’t seen it, do yourself a favor and watch it immediately. it’s a modern classic in every sense.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.31561928987503